sections.body:
Lessons 4 and 5 added behaviour by wrapping objects. That needs an interface or a non-final class, and it misses self-calls whenever the proxy forwards. It also only works on objects you create through the proxy. Sometimes you need more: timing a method inside a library jar, or tracing a framework class you don't construct yourself. An **agent** gets access to the class instead of the object.

An agent is an ordinary class with a special entry point. The JVM calls it with a `java.lang.instrument.Instrumentation`, the JDK's handle for watching and changing classes. There are three ways to start one:

- `java -javaagent:agent.jar[=args]` calls the agent's `premain(String args, Instrumentation inst)` before your `main`. You can repeat the option, and the agents start in the order given.
- An executable jar can name an agent in its manifest with `Launcher-Agent-Class`. The JVM calls its `agentmain` before the application's `main`.
- A tool can attach to a JVM that is already running and load an agent jar. The JVM calls `agentmain(String, Instrumentation)`. The next section covers this case.

The agent jar's manifest names the class: `Premain-Class` or `Agent-Class`. `Can-Retransform-Classes: true` asks for the right to change classes that are already loaded.

The first thing most agents do is register a `ClassFileTransformer`. The JVM calls its `transform` method with the bytes of every class it is about to define from then on, and the method returns either new bytes or `null`, which means "no change". The smallest useful agent changes nothing and only watches:

```java
public static void premain(String args, Instrumentation inst) {
    inst.addTransformer(new ClassFileTransformer() {
        @Override
        public byte[] transform(ClassLoader loader, String className, Class<?> redefined,
                                ProtectionDomain domain, byte[] bytes) {
            System.out.println("[agent] loading " + className + " (" + bytes.length + " bytes)");
            return null;                                   // null: leave the class as it is
        }
    });
}
```

```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun premain(args: String?, inst: Instrumentation) {
    inst.addTransformer(object : ClassFileTransformer {
        override fun transform(
            loader: ClassLoader?, className: String?, redefined: Class<*>?,
            domain: ProtectionDomain?, bytes: ByteArray,
        ): ByteArray? {
            println("[agent] loading $className (${bytes.size} bytes)")
            return null                                    // null: leave the class as it is
        }
    })
}
```

The class files that `transform` receives are the bytes from lesson 6. An agent that wants to change a class rewrites them, with the ClassFile API on JDK 24 and later, or with a library such as Byte Buddy. A startup agent sees almost every class of your application, because nearly all of them load after `premain` returns. Note `ClassFileTransformer` uses an anonymous class above, not a lambda: both of its `transform` methods are default methods, so it isn't a functional interface.

